# TypeScript: No `as any`, idiomatic typing

- Never use `as any` in production code.
- Prefer precise types, discriminated unions, and generics over type erasure.
- For unknown input, use `unknown` and narrow with type guards.
- Prefer `satisfies` to validate object shapes without widening.
- Avoid non-null assertions (`!`) unless there is a proven invariant and a short comment.

## Preferred patterns

```ts
// ❌ BAD
const payload = data as any;
process(payload.value);

// ✅ GOOD
type Payload = { value: string };
const payload = data as Payload;
process(payload.value);
```

```ts
// ✅ BETTER FOR UNTRUSTED DATA
function isPayload(input: unknown): input is { value: string } {
  return typeof input === "object" && input !== null && "value" in input;
}
```

If a cast is unavoidable, cast to the narrowest valid type and document why.
